
Description

This program demonstrates the creation of multiple child processes using the fork() system call.
The parent process creates three child processes.
Each child process prints its process ID, waits for a different amount of time using sleep(), and then terminates.
The parent process uses wait() to wait for any one child process to complete.
The parent then uses waitpid() to wait for a specific child process to complete.
Finally, the parent waits for the remaining child process and terminates after all children have completed.

System Calls Used

fork() is used to create a new child process.
wait() is used by the parent to wait for any child process to complete.
waitpid() is used by the parent to wait for a specific child process.
sleep() is used to create different execution times for the child processes.
exit() is used to terminate the child process.
getpid() is used to obtain the process ID of the child.

Comparison of wait() and waitpid()

wait() waits for any one child process that has completed.
waitpid() waits for a particular child process whose process ID is specified.
wait() is useful when the parent does not need to select a specific child.
waitpid() is useful when the parent needs to synchronize with a particular child.



